const body = `<h1>Unit 4: Iteration (Loops)</h1>

<p>
    Your programs so far do things <strong>once</strong>. One attack roll. One character
    sheet. One tip calculation. But games loop: combat rounds repeat until someone falls.
    You explore room after room. Your inventory has multiple items. You roll damage for
    every attack.
</p>

<p>
    This unit teaches <strong>repetition</strong>: while loops, for loops, and nested
    loops. By the end, you'll build a <strong>Dungeon Crawler</strong> — combat rounds,
    room-by-room exploration, and an inventory system, all wrapping the code you've built
    across Units 1–3.
</p>

<h2>Concepts</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Topic</th><th>What you'll learn</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="while-loops.html">while Loops</a></td>
        <td>Repeat while a condition is true. Combat rounds, sentinel patterns.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="for-loops.html">for Loops</a></td>
        <td>Count-controlled repetition. Dungeon rooms, inventory iteration.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="string-loops.html">Loops + Strings</a></td>
        <td>Traversing strings. Finding, counting, and transforming characters.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="nested-loops.html">Nested Loops</a></td>
        <td>Loops inside loops. Dungeon grid, multiplication tables, patterns.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="loop-analysis.html">Tracing Loops</a></td>
        <td>Trace tables. How many times does this loop run? Common off-by-one bugs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="run-time-analysis.html">Run-Time Analysis</a></td>
        <td>Linear vs quadratic, step counting, log n, pricing the classics. New CED 2.12.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>No teacher demos for this unit.</strong> Unit 4 is all hands-on exploration.
    The RPG guides below are the primary build — the teacher works through one with you,
    then you tackle the other on your own.
</div>

<h2>Your RPG Project</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Guide</th><th>What you'll build</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="dungeon-system.html">Dungeon Crawl (Student RPG Guide)</a></td>
        <td>Combat rounds, room exploration, inventory, boss fight. Loops wrapping everything from Units 1–3.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="dungeon-crawl.html">Dungeon Crawl Extended (Student RPG Guide)</a></td>
        <td>Multi-floor dungeon, trap rooms, escape mechanics, treasure rooms. More loops, more decisions.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>RPG Thread</h2>

<p>
    Loops are how your RPG stays alive. The combat system from Unit 3 was one round —
    now a while loop makes it multi-round until someone falls. For loops generate room
    after room in the dungeon. In Unit 6, you'll loop through inventory arrays. In Unit 8,
    nested loops traverse the dungeon grid. Unit 10 recursion replaces some of these loops.
</p>

<h2>Prerequisites</h2>

<p>Before starting this unit, you should be comfortable with:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Primitive types, variables, and arithmetic: <a href="../unit-01/index.html">Unit 1</a></li>
    <li>Objects, Scanner, and String methods: <a href="../unit-02/index.html">Unit 2</a></li>
    <li>Boolean expressions and if statements: <a href="../unit-03/index.html">Unit 3</a></li>
</ul>

<h2>Reference Materials</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Quick Reference:</strong> <a href="../reference/unit-04-quickref.html">Iteration Quick Ref</a> &mdash; while/for syntax, loop patterns, trace tables, run-time analysis</li>
</ul>

<h2>Assessment</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Part A:</strong> Knowledge — while vs for, loop headers, break/continue, nested loops</li>
    <li><strong>Part B:</strong> Code trace — trace tables, predict loop output, count iterations</li>
    <li><strong>Part C:</strong> Write code — implement a loop from a specification, fix an off-by-one bug</li>
</ul>`;
